            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The case Tarrant Regional Water District v. Herrmann concerns the interpretation of an interstate water\u2011compact that governs how water can be allocated among states."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "65ce80bc475305c4194168ad7121e63e3ac2be6cf60e641e50f9b9f700fd5c8c",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-65ce80bc4753-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Compact says each state may manage water rights under its own laws, but only as water is available under the Compact."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. O'Connell",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "65ce80bc475305c4194168ad7121e63e3ac2be6cf60e641e50f9b9f700fd5c8c",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-65ce80bc4753-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. O'Connell, one side argues that any state law that conflicts with the Compact\u2019s allocation should be preempted."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "65ce80bc475305c4194168ad7121e63e3ac2be6cf60e641e50f9b9f700fd5c8c",
